#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f806240-4d0c-5c85-b338-51b52df8ece9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Additional Linear Term"}] :question/id #uuid "4e5af199-faf5-540b-ad83-c3090e761183" :question/math-academy-id "e-21230" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2$." :question/worked-solution "To solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2\n$$\n\nwe raise both sides to the power of $2$. This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x + 10} &= x - 2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{x + 10})^{2} &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\nx + 10 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 6) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1, 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n$\\sqrt{-1 + 10}|= ^(?) - 1 - 2; \\sqrt{9}|= ^(?) - 3; 3 \\mid \\ne - 3\\cdot$\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=6$ is a solution of the original equation:\n$\\sqrt{6 + 10}|= ^(?)6 - 2; \\sqrt{16}|= ^(?)4; 4|= 4✓$\nIndeed, the value $x=6$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2\n$$\n\nis $x=6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054563 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"}] :question/id #uuid "18c11d40-eed5-5af6-ade4-70e024af6cd1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5232" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2$." :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x - 6} &= 2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x - 6})^{2} &= 2^{2} \\\\\n2x - 6 &= 4 \\\\\n2x &= 10 \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2(5) - 6} &= ^(?)2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} &= ^(?)2 \\\\\n2 &= 2✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2\n$$\n\nis $x=5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054564 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "1d21fa8f-95d6-55b7-916f-b12d00f2f002" :question/math-academy-id "e-5233" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\n1. $\\sqrt{5-x} = -2$\n2. $\\sqrt{4-x} = 5$\n3. $\\sqrt{2x+1} = -1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054565 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/id #uuid "2c646210-4559-5e42-9feb-05428d1f674c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5234" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8} - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8})^{2} &= (2)^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x + 8 &= 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 4)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n&= x = 4, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=1$, we get\n$\\sqrt{1^{2} - 5(1) + 8} - 2|= ^(?)0; \\sqrt{4}|= ^(?)2; 2|= 2.✓$\n- For $x=4$, we get\n$\\sqrt{4^{2} - 5(4) + 8} - 2|= ^(?)0; \\sqrt{4}|= ^(?)2; 2|= 2.✓$\n\nBoth $x=1$ and $x=4$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0\n$$\n\nare $x=1$ and $x=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e628d1de-d244-5147-9b9f-c225b0748668" :question/math-academy-id "q-114606" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. - $\\sqrt{7-2x}=-2$\nII. - $\\sqrt{x-3}=-7$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{6x-2}=5$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and II state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and II have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f806240-4d0c-5c85-b338-51b52df8ece9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Additional Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4eea85b-9075-5288-b792-fa1024e716b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052738} {:answer/id #uuid "cb870bc3-9c39-5ab8-bf2a-7759f5b162e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052739} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3ea794-f633-5461-b0e8-b125f1fb5a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052740} {:answer/id #uuid "234d496e-035f-5c1d-96d7-a9bc90e9538c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052741} {:answer/id #uuid "678b637f-5662-5617-ae66-05cea6d59450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052742}] :answer-field/id #uuid "46723694-ff57-5a32-a595-df0dbc65a0fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052737}] :question/id #uuid "d7893b3a-653a-5126-aece-84c455846266" :question/math-academy-id "q-39659" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ if given that $\\sqrt{14 - x} = x - 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5ea5f9e-a16b-5641-85c0-e621b0c63560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052756} {:answer/id #uuid "c10cb34c-2432-504c-b33e-eb232521d6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052757} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd6a8a7-95b7-55c3-bd98-3412e97dd258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052758} {:answer/id #uuid "1d80fc2b-952d-5d97-b472-408f474f4301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052759} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf3501f-92bf-515c-9d1d-42bda7276ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052760}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f36c2096-a42c-5241-9914-d9b637a3c9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052755}] :question/id #uuid "fceba246-1aa7-568a-aca1-de613dbe563a" :question/math-academy-id "q-39822" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{1 - 7x} =-3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28c9adff-f6cc-5086-b62e-6ff879b21f10" :question/math-academy-id "q-39830" :question/problem "Solve $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the term $-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}$ by subtracting $8$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $-6$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2} & =12 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} & ={12}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =144 \\\\ x & =142.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{142+2}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{\\sqrt{144}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{12}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=142$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6$  is $x=142.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90cf7ef5-e6f8-59ba-bac9-68370011b547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054058} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe583c1-a65f-54fd-8332-64915e9f44e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054059} {:answer/id #uuid "df8486f1-b7e4-548a-9116-25d08e0731cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2, 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054060} {:answer/id #uuid "0deb0ec9-f060-51a2-ace1-6397c70e84c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054061} {:answer/id #uuid "6708f399-2dc0-55c3-ab86-f86dd2bbd144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054062}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fd2c8fe6-ab40-5aa6-807d-8e4d83bc02e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054057}] :question/id #uuid "a775f77a-5da2-50d5-b383-16e8